      <h3 id="virt-editing-vm-dynamic-key-injection_dynamic-key" class="group scroll-mt-20">Enabling dynamic SSH key injection by using the web console<a href="#virt-editing-vm-dynamic-key-injection_dynamic-key" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">You can enable dynamic key injection for a virtual machine (VM) by using the OpenShift Container Platform web console. Then, you can update the public SSH key at runtime.</p>
      <p>The key is added to the VM by the QEMU guest agent, which is installed with Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL) 9 and later.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul><li>The guest operating system is RHEL 9 or later.</li></ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>Navigate to <strong>Virtualization</strong> → <strong>VirtualMachines</strong> in the web console.</li>
      <li>Select a VM to open the <strong>VirtualMachine details</strong> page.</li>
      <li>On the <strong>Configuration</strong> tab, click <strong>Scripts</strong>.</li>
      <li>
      If you have not already added a public SSH key to your project, click the edit icon beside <strong>Authorized SSH key</strong> and select one of the following options:
      <ul>
      <li><strong>Use existing</strong>: Select a secret from the secrets list.</li>
      <li>
      <strong>Add new</strong>:
      <ol>
      <li>Browse to the SSH key file or paste the file in the key field.</li>
      <li>Enter the secret name.</li>
      <li>Optional: Select <strong>Automatically apply this key to any new VirtualMachine you create in this project</strong>.</li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>Set <strong>Dynamic SSH key injection</strong> to on.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Save</strong>.</li>
      </ol>
      <h3 id="virt-enabling-dynamic-key-injection-cli_dynamic-key" class="group scroll-mt-20">Enabling dynamic key injection by using the CLI<a href="#virt-enabling-dynamic-key-injection-cli_dynamic-key" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">You can enable dynamic key injection for a virtual machine (VM) by using the command line. Then, you can update the public SSH key at runtime.</p>
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>Only Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL) 9 supports dynamic key injection.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p>The key is added to the VM by the QEMU guest agent, which is installed automatically with RHEL 9.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You generated an SSH key pair by running the <code>ssh-keygen</code> command.</li>
      <li>You have installed the OpenShift CLI (<code>oc</code>).</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a manifest file for a <code>VirtualMachine</code> object and a <code>Secret</code> object.
      <p>Example manifest:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1\nkind: VirtualMachine\nmetadata:\n  name: example-vm\n  namespace: example-namespace\nspec:\n  dataVolumeTemplates:\n    - metadata:\n        name: example-vm-volume\n      spec:\n        sourceRef:\n          kind: DataSource\n          name: rhel9\n          namespace: openshift-virtualization-os-images\n        storage:\n          resources: {}\n  instancetype:\n    name: u1.medium\n  preference:\n    name: rhel.9\n  runStrategy: Always\n  template:\n    spec:\n      domain:\n        devices: {}\n      volumes:\n        - dataVolume:\n            name: example-vm-volume\n          name: rootdisk\n        - cloudInitNoCloud:\n            userData: |-\n              #cloud-config\n              runcmd:\n              - [ setsebool, -P, virt_qemu_ga_manage_ssh, on ]\n          name: cloudinitdisk\n      accessCredentials:\n        - sshPublicKey:\n            propagationMethod:\n              qemuGuestAgent:\n                users: [\"cloud-user\"]\n            source:\n              secret:\n                secretName: authorized-keys\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Secret\nmetadata:\n  name: authorized-keys\ndata:\n  key: c3NoLXJzYSB..."} lang="yaml" />
      <ul>
      <li><code>spec.template.spec.volumes.cloudInitNoCloud</code> defines the data source, for example <code>userData</code>.</li>
      <li><code>spec.template.spec.accessCredentials.sshPublicKey.source.secret.secretName</code> defines the <code>secret</code> object name.</li>
      <li><code>data.key</code> within the <code>secret</code> object defines the full public SSH key.</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the <code>VirtualMachine</code> and <code>Secret</code> objects by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f <manifest_file>.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Start the VM by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ virtctl start vm example-vm -n example-namespace"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      Get the VM configuration by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc describe vm example-vm -n example-namespace"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Example output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1\nkind: VirtualMachine\nmetadata:\n  name: example-vm\n  namespace: example-namespace\nspec:\n  template:\n    spec:\n      accessCredentials:\n        - sshPublicKey:\n            propagationMethod:\n              qemuGuestAgent:\n                users: [\"cloud-user\"]\n            source:\n              secret:\n                secretName: authorized-keys\n# ..."} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      </ul>
      <h3 id="virt-using-virtctl-ssh-command_dynamic-key" class="group scroll-mt-20">Using the virtctl ssh command<a href="#virt-using-virtctl-ssh-command_dynamic-key" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">You can use the <code>virtctl ssh</code> command to access a running virtual machine instance (VMI). The command accepts VM or VMI targets.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You installed the <code>virtctl</code> command-line tool.</li>
      <li>You added a public SSH key to the VM.</li>
      <li>You have an SSH client installed.</li>
      <li>The environment where you installed the <code>virtctl</code> tool has the cluster permissions required to access the VM. For example, you ran <code>oc login</code> or you set the <code>KUBECONFIG</code> environment variable.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Run the <code>virtctl ssh</code> command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ virtctl -n <namespace> ssh <username>@vm/<vm_name> -i <ssh_key>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>You must specify the resource type (<code>vmi/</code> or <code>vm/</code>) before the VM name.</p>
      <p>For example:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ virtctl -n my-namespace ssh cloud-user@vm/example-vm -i my-key"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="virt-using-virtctl-port-forward-command_dynamic-key" class="group scroll-mt-20">Using the virtctl port-forward command<a href="#virt-using-virtctl-port-forward-command_dynamic-key" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">You can use your local OpenSSH client and the <code>virtctl port-forward</code> command to connect to a running virtual machine (VM). You can use this method with Ansible to automate the configuration of VMs.</p>
      <p>This method is recommended for low-traffic applications because port-forwarding traffic is sent over the control plane. This method is not recommended for high-traffic applications such as Rsync or Remote Desktop Protocol because it places a heavy burden on the API server.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You have installed the <code>virtctl</code> client.</li>
      <li>The virtual machine you want to access is running.</li>
      <li>The environment where you installed the <code>virtctl</code> tool has the cluster permissions required to access the VM. For example, you ran <code>oc login</code> or you set the <code>KUBECONFIG</code> environment variable.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Add the following text to the <code>~/.ssh/config</code> file on your client machine:
      <CodeBlock code={"Host vm/*\n  ProxyCommand virtctl port-forward --stdio=true %h %p"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Connect to the VM by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ ssh <user>@vm/<vm_name>.<namespace>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
